grund refs — list every citation site of a coordinate, as `path:line`, so you can
see who depends on a declaration before you change it.

Usage:  grund refs <ID>[.<section>] [PATH] [--section S] [--descendants]
                   [--summary] [--total] [--format text|json]
        grund refs --glob PATTERN [--glob …] [PATH] [--summary] [--total] [--format text|json]
        grund refs --from COORDINATE [PATH] [--summary] [--total] [--format text|json]

With no PATH, the run covers the whole project or workspace discovered from the current directory; a PATH narrows it.

With a `.<section>` (or --section), only citations of that exact section are
listed; --descendants widens that to the section and every section beneath it.
An ID with no citations prints nothing and exits 0, or the line `not cited`
under --total.

--glob lists every site whose cited coordinate a pattern matches, dangling ones
included; repeated, the patterns union and each site is listed once. A bare
`--glob 'FS-login'` selects only the bare citations of FS-login, where
`grund refs FS-login` is `--glob 'FS-login{,.**}'`. --from asks the other way,
what one coordinate cites: the sites written in its lead, its ancestors and
itself, never in a descendant, or the line `cites nothing` under --total.
Under either, every positional is PATH.

Options:
  --section S          list only citations of that section path   e.g. grund refs FS-login --section 3
  --descendants        also list citations of sections beneath it e.g. grund refs FS-login.3 --descendants
  --glob PATTERN       sites citing what it matches; repeatable   e.g. grund refs --glob 'FS-login.**'
                       not with --from, --section or --descendants.
  --from COORDINATE    what that coordinate's own body cites      e.g. grund refs --from FS-login.3
                       given once; not with --glob, --section or --descendants.
  --summary            group citations by citing file             e.g. grund refs FS-login --summary
  --total              the set's size instead of its members      e.g. grund refs FS-login --total
  --format text|json   text (default) prints `path:line: <citation>`; json emits NDJSON.
  --path-base project|invocation
                       project (default) spells report paths from the config root;
                       invocation: relative to the path you passed, or the current directory if you passed none.
                       e.g. grund refs FS-login src/ --path-base=invocation
                       --path-base=invocation replaces a version-1 `[output] relative_paths = false`.
                       --format json replaces a version-1 `[output] format = "json"`; a flag outranks its key.

The citation list is the result, so it goes to stdout (text and json alike) —
`grund refs <ID> | …` works like `grund list`. Only a `note:` goes to stderr:
one for an ID declared nowhere, or, with no PATH and no section, one naming
`grund list --unused` for a declared ID nothing cites.

Exit:  0 scan succeeded (with or without hits) · 1 ID or --from source rejected after context
       · 2 run/scan error, or a refused --glob.

Examples:
  grund refs FS-login             # every citation of FS-login
  grund refs FS-login --summary   # one row per citing file
  grund refs FS-login --total     # how many sites, in how many files
  grund refs FS-login.3           # only citations of section 3
  grund refs FS-login --format json # one NDJSON record per citation site
  grund refs --glob 'FS-login.{lockout,reset}' # who cites either section
  grund refs --from FS-login.3    # what FS-login.3 cites, and where
